  "response": "## Lead finding: `Invoice` and `User` are each declared twice, and the two halves disagree\n\n`crimes scan` reports two Contract Split-Brain findings, both anchored in `src/api/state.ts` with `src/billing.ts` as the related file:\n\n| | A — `src/api/state.ts` | B — `src/billing.ts` | disagreements |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `Invoice` (crime_00012) | L9, 2 fields | L19, 11 fields | 6 — `userId`, `subtotalCents`, `discountCents`, `taxCents`, `currency`, `createdAt` |\n| `User` (crime_00011) | L4, 2 fields | L4, 5 fields | 1 — `createdAt` (plus `plan`, `country` below the detector's critical-field bar) |\n\nEvery disagreement is one-directional: A is a strict subset of B. Confidence 0.82 on both, driven by 100% field overlap of the smaller declaration — the two names reduce to the same concept and every field A has, B also has. The detector's own summary states the mechanism: *nothing in the build checks the two declarations against each other*. I confirmed why — neither file imports the other, and **none of the four interfaces is exported**. There is no edge for `tsc` to check across, which is also why `blast_radius: 0` on both findings. That zero is accurate and should not be read as reassurance; the risk here is duplication, not propagation.\n\n## Which file an agent edits\n\n**`src/billing.ts`** — for `Invoice`, with high confidence.\n\n`src/billing.ts:168` is the only place in the repo an `Invoice` is actually constructed, inside `generateInvoice`. An agent adding, say, `refundedCents` has to make the field get *populated*, and the search for \"where does an Invoice get built\" lands there and nowhere else. The scan corroborates the gravitational pull: eight findings sit in `billing.ts` (crimes 00009, 00014, 00015, 00028, 00032, 00033, 00036, 00039) versus two declaration-only findings in `state.ts`. That's where the behaviour is, so that's the file that gets loaded.\n\nThe type checker then rewards the wrong-but-partial edit. Adding a required field to B's `Invoice` errors at exactly one site — L168 — which the agent fixes in the same breath, and typecheck goes green. Adding it to A's `Invoice` would error at zero sites, because both consumers (`invoice: Invoice[] = []` at L27, `invoices: Invoice[] = []` at L34) are empty arrays. So the file that is easier to forget is also the file that never complains.\n\n`User` inverts and is worse. B's `User` appears only in parameter and return positions (L40, L51, L272) — no object literal in the file — so adding a required field to B produces **no local error at all**. A's `User` is constructed twice with literals (L24, L33), so A is the only side that would have complained. An agent editing B for `User` gets a clean build with nothing pointing at `state.ts`.\n\n## What silently breaks\n\nNothing at build time. That is the failure.\n\n`src/api/state.ts` keeps shipping a 2-field `Invoice` and a 2-field `User` that now omit one more field than they did before the edit. The gap is invisible in both directions that matter:\n\n- **Structural subtyping actively conceals it.** B's `User` (5 fields) is assignable anywhere A's `User` (2 fields) is expected — the extra fields are silently dropped from the contract's view. No error is available to raise.\n- **The loud direction never fires.** A's `User` is *not* assignable to B's — it's missing `plan`, `country`, `createdAt` — but that error can only occur if the two declarations meet, and they cannot, because neither is exported.\n\nConcretely: the new field is persisted and computed on the `billing.ts` path, and absent from the `api/state.ts` path. Anything that later serializes or validates through the A-shaped declaration — which is the one under `src/api/`, i.e. the wire-facing name — emits a record missing the field, with the compiler asserting it is fine. The pre-existing 6-field `Invoice` gap is the proof this already happened once: `userId`, `currency`, and four money fields are known to `billing.ts` and unknown to `api/state.ts` today.\n\n## Where this sits, by `scores.agent_risk`\n\n| finding | agent_risk | severity | size |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| contract_drift `Invoice` (00012) | **0.37** | 0.96 | 9-line block |\n| contract_drift `User` (00011) | **0.37** | 0.90 | 4-line block |\n| large_function `generateInvoice` (00033) | **0.13** | 0.90 | 204 lines |\n| large_file `billing.ts` (00036) | **0.07** | 0.60 | 310 lines |\n\nBoth drift findings outrank both structural findings on `agent_risk` — 2.8× the God Function, 5.3× the God File — and that ordering survives precisely because it is not built from severity or line count. Severity ties `User` drift with the God Function at 0.90 and would tell you nothing. Line count reverses the ranking outright: 204 and 310 lines against two blocks of 4 and 9.\n\n`agent_risk` separates them because it scores how likely an agent's edit goes wrong *and stays undetected*. `generateInvoice` is large but self-announcing — it is one contiguous body, an agent that opens the file sees the whole thing, and a mistake made in it surfaces at the single place it lives. Same for the 310-line file: the cost is context budget, not correctness. Contract drift is the opposite shape. Two small blocks in two files an agent has no reason to open together, no import edge to surface the second one, and a failure mode that is a *correct-looking edit in the right file*. Size is what makes a change expensive; split-brain is what makes it wrong.\n\nOne honest caveat on the ordering. 0.37 is not the top of this report — `direct_date` in `src/date.ts` scores 0.45, `return_shape_roulette` 0.44, `missing_agent_context` 0.43. Contract drift ranks 11th and 12th of 39 on `agent_risk` alone. Its claim to the lead is the combination: the two highest-severity findings in the repo (0.96, 0.90) whose agent_risk also clears every structural finding. Fix these before you split the file or extract the function — refactoring `billing.ts` while two contracts disagree just distributes the disagreement across more files.\n\n**Fix shape** (from the findings' own `suggested_actions`): keep B as the single declaration, export it, and have A reference it as a named projection — `type User = Pick<BillingUser, \"id\" | \"email\">` — so the next field addition either reaches both or fails the build. That converts a silent divergence into a compiler error, which is the only durable version of this fix.",
